Introduction {#intro}
=====================

	This module defines CSS features relating to [=scroll chaining=], [=boundary default actions=], and [=overscroll areas=] positioned beyond the [=scroll boundary=] of a container.
	It supersedes and extends [[!CSS-OVERSCROLL-1]], and is organized into the following sections:

	: [[#overscroll-containers-and-areas|Overscroll Containers and Areas]]
	::
		Defines [=overscroll containers=], [=overscroll areas=], the 'overscroll-container-type' property,
		the '':overscroll-open'' and '':overscroll-closed'' pseudo-classes, and the ''::overscroll-backdrop'' pseudo-element.

	: [[#box-model|The Box Model]]
	::
		Defines the anonymous [=overscroll area parent=] [=scroll container=] box, out-of-flow positioning
		and mandatory scroll snapping of [=overscroll areas=], painting order, and push vs. overlay translation.

	: [[#scroll-chaining-and-boundary-default-actions|Scroll Chaining and Boundary Default Actions]] and [[#overscroll-behavior-properties|Overscroll Behavior Properties]]
	::
		Defines [=scroll chaining=], the [=scroll chain=] order for [=overscroll containers=] and [=overscroll areas=],
		[=boundary default actions=], and the 'overscroll-behavior' property and its physical and flow-relative longhands.

	: [[#overscroll-events|Overscroll Events]]
	::
		Defines the {{OverscrollEvent}} interface and the {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollstart}},
		{{OverscrollEvent/overscrollchanging}}, {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollend}}, and {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollcancel}} events.

	: [[#html-spec-changes|Appendix A: HTML Specification Changes]]
	::
		Defines the HTML <a attr-for="html-global">overscrollcontainer</a> and <a attr-for="html-global">overscrollarea</a> attributes,
		the <code>toggle-overscroll</code>, <code>show-overscroll</code>, and <code>hide-overscroll</code> command invokers,
		container-scoped [=inert=] rules, and {{GlobalEventHandlers}} additions.

Value Definitions {#values}
---------------------------

	This specification follows the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/CSS2/about.html#property-defs">CSS property definition conventions</a> from [[!CSS2]]
	using the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/css-values-3/#value-defs">value definition syntax</a> from [[!CSS-VALUES-3]].
	Value types not defined in this specification are defined in CSS Values &amp; Units [[!CSS-VALUES-3]].
	Combination with other CSS modules may expand the definitions of these value types.

	In addition to the property-specific values listed in their definitions,
	all properties defined in this specification
	also accept the <a>CSS-wide keywords</a> as their property value.
	For readability they have not been repeated explicitly.

Module Interactions {#placement}
--------------------------------

	This module extends scrolling and overflow concepts defined in [[!CSS-OVERFLOW-3]], [[!CSS-SCROLL-SNAP-1]], [[!CSS-SCROLL-SNAP-2]], [[!CSS-POSITION-4]], and [[!CSSOM-VIEW-1]].


Overscroll Containers and Areas {#overscroll-containers-and-areas}
==================================================================

Overscroll Containers {#overscroll-containers}
----------------------------------------------

An <dfn export>overscroll container</dfn> is an element that:

1. Generates a [=principal box=] whose [=inner display type=] is not ''display/table'', and that is neither an <a spec="css-display-3">internal table box</a>, an <a spec="css-display-3">internal ruby box</a>, nor a <a spec="css-display-3" lt="atomic inline">non-atomic</a> <a spec="css-display-3">inline-level</a> box;
2. Has the host-language overscroll container attribute (in HTML, <a attr-for="html-global">overscrollcontainer</a>; see [[#html-attributes]]); and
3. Has a computed 'overscroll-container-type' other than ''overscroll-container-type/none''.

Note: An [=overscroll container=] does not have to be a [=scroll container=] (its 'overflow' may be ''overflow/visible'').

Overscroll Areas {#overscroll-areas}
------------------------------------

An element |area| is an <dfn export>overscroll area</dfn> if all of the following are true:

1. |area| has the host-language overscroll area attribute (in HTML, <a attr-for="html-global">overscrollarea</a>; see [[#html-attributes]]);
2. |area|'s [=flat tree=] parent element is an [=overscroll container=] in the same [=tree scope=] as |area|;
3. |area| is not [=in the top layer=]; and
4. |area| is the target of at least one [=show invoker=] or [=toggle invoker=] in the same [=tree scope=] as |area| (see [[#html-invoker-commands]]).

If any of these conditions ceases to hold, |area| ceases to be an [=overscroll area=] and is laid out as an ordinary child of its parent.

<dfn export lt="open an overscroll area|opening an overscroll area|opening|opened">Opening</dfn> (or <dfn export lt="reveal|revealing|revealed">revealing</dfn>) an [=overscroll area=] brings the [=overscroll area=] into its [=overscroll container=]'s [=scrollport=] by scrolling its [=overscroll area parent=], whether via user [=scroll chaining=] or by activating an [=overscroll invoker=].

The 'overscroll-container-type' Property {#overscroll-container-type-property}
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

<pre class=propdef>
Name: overscroll-container-type
Value: auto | push | overlay | none
Initial: auto
Applies to: all elements
Inherited: no
Percentages: N/A
Media: visual
Computed value: specified keyword
Animation type: discrete
Canonical order: <abbr title="follows order of property value definition">per grammar</abbr>
</pre>

The 'overscroll-container-type' property specifies whether an element with the host-language overscroll container attribute establishes an [=overscroll container=], and how [=opening=] its [=overscroll areas=] affects the container's content.

<dl dfn-for="overscroll-container-type" dfn-type="value">
	<dt><dfn>auto</dfn>
	<dd>
		Behaves as ''overscroll-container-type/push''.

		Note: The definition of ''overscroll-container-type/auto'' may change as usage experience is gained.

	<dt><dfn>push</dfn>
	<dd>
		The element can establish an [=overscroll container=]. [=Opening=] an [=overscroll area=] translates the container's content alongside the [=overscroll area=] (see [[#overscroll-stacking-and-push]]).

	<dt><dfn>overlay</dfn>
	<dd>
		The element can establish an [=overscroll container=]. [=Opening=] an [=overscroll area=] translates only the [=overscroll area=], overlaying it on top of the stationary container content (see [[#overscroll-stacking-and-push]]).

	<dt><dfn>none</dfn>
	<dd>
		The element does not establish an [=overscroll container=].
</dl>

<div class=example>
	An [=overscroll area=] positioned outside the inline-start edge of an [=overscroll container=], with a media query opting out via ''overscroll-container-type: none'' on wide viewports so <code>#drawer</code> lays out as an inline-block side panel:

	<pre class="lang-html">
	&lt;button commandfor="drawer" command="toggle-overscroll"&gt;Menu&lt;/button&gt;
	&lt;div id="container" overscrollcontainer&gt;
		&lt;nav id="drawer" overscrollarea&gt;...&lt;/nav&gt;
		&lt;main&gt;...&lt;/main&gt;
	&lt;/div&gt;
	</pre>

	<pre class="lang-css">
	#container {
		overscroll-container-type: overlay;
		overflow: auto;
	}
	#drawer {
		display: inline-block;
		right: 100%;
		width: 250px;
		height: 100%;
	}
	@media (min-width: 900px) {
		#container {
			overscroll-container-type: none;
		}
	}
	</pre>
</div>

The '':overscroll-open'' and '':overscroll-closed'' Pseudo-Classes {#overscroll-open-pseudo}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The <dfn selector>:overscroll-open</dfn> pseudo-class matches an [=overscroll area=] whose [=overscroll area parent=] is in the [=overscrolling state=] (see [[#overscroll-event-dispatch]]).

The <dfn selector>:overscroll-closed</dfn> pseudo-class matches an [=overscroll area=] whose [=overscroll area parent=] is not in the [=overscrolling state=].

Note: An element that is not a valid [=overscroll area=] (for example, when its parent has ''overscroll-container-type: none'' or when it lacks a valid [=show invoker=] or [=toggle invoker=]) matches neither '':overscroll-open'' nor '':overscroll-closed''.

The ''::overscroll-backdrop'' Pseudo-Element {#overscroll-backdrop-pseudo}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each [=overscroll area=] originates a <dfn selector>::overscroll-backdrop</dfn> [=fully styleable pseudo-element=].

When generated, the ''::overscroll-backdrop'' box is a child of its [=originating element=]'s [=overscroll area parent=], painted immediately behind the [=overscroll area=]. Its [=containing block=] is the [=fixed containing block=] of the [=overscroll area parent=], so it remains aligned with that [=overscroll area parent=]'s [=scrollport=] rather than scrolling with the [=overscroll area=].

Note: Because ''::overscroll-backdrop'' uses the [=fixed containing block=] of its own [=overscroll area parent=], scrolling that [=overscroll area parent=] to [=open=] its [=overscroll area=] does not move the backdrop. However, in ''overscroll-container-type/push'' mode, if another [=overscroll area=] higher in the stack is [=opened=], that higher [=overscroll area parent=]'s scroll translation shifts this [=overscroll area parent=] and its ''::overscroll-backdrop'' along with the rest of the underlying content.


The Box Model {#box-model}
==========================

Containment and Clipping on the Overscroll Container {#overscroll-container-box}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

An [=overscroll container=] establishes [=layout containment=] (as if ''contain: layout'' were applied) and clips its [=overscroll area parent=] boxes to its [=scrollport=].

The Overscroll Area Parent Box {#overscroll-area-parent-box}
------------------------------------------------------------

For each [=overscroll area=] |area| of an [=overscroll container=] |container|, the user agent generates an anonymous [=scroll container=] box called |area|'s <dfn export>overscroll area parent</dfn>:

1. |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=] is a layout child of |container|'s [=principal box=]. It is out-of-flow positioned (''position: absolute'', ''inset: 0'', ''display: block'') with |container|'s [=fixed containing block=] as its [=containing block=].
2. The [=overscroll area parent=] establishes a [=scroll container=] (''overflow: auto'', ''scrollbar-width: none'', ''pointer-events: none'') that inherits 'overscroll-behavior' and 'scroll-behavior' from |area|.
	Unlike standard [=scroll containers=], its [=scrollable overflow area=] is not clamped at the [=scroll origin=]; it extends in all four physical directions (top, left, bottom, right) to enclose both its [=local containing block=] origin (<code>(0, 0)</code> of size equal to |container|'s [=scrollport=]) and |area|'s border box.
3. |area|'s ''::overscroll-backdrop'' box and |area|'s [=principal box=] are reparented as the children of |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=], in that order.

Note: The [=overscroll area parent=] can be implemented as a non-exposed pseudo-element originating on the [=overscroll area=] (which inherits 'overscroll-behavior' and 'scroll-behavior' from the [=overscroll area=]), subject to the box tree reparenting above.

Positioning and Snapping of Overscroll Areas {#overscroll-area-positioning}
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every [=overscroll area=] |area| is laid out and snapped within its [=overscroll area parent=] |scroller| as follows:

1. If |area|'s specified 'position' is not ''position/absolute'' or ''position/fixed'', its computed 'position' is ''position/absolute''.
	If |area|'s specified 'display' is ''display/contents'', its computed 'display' is ''display/block''; otherwise its 'display' is [=blockified=].
	|area|'s [=containing block=] is |scroller|'s [=local containing block=].
2. |scroller| is a [=scroll snap container=] (''scroll-snap-type: pair mandatory'') that ignores 'scroll-snap-align' on descendants and establishes two [=scroll snap areas=]:
	* An initial [=scroll snap area=] equal to the rectangle of |scroller|'s [=scrollport=] at scroll offset <code>(0, 0)</code>, with ''scroll-snap-align: center'' and ''scroll-snap-stop: always''. This is |scroller|'s [=initial scroll position=].
	* A target [=scroll snap area=] equal to |area|'s border box, with ''scroll-snap-align: center'' and ''scroll-snap-stop: normal''.

Note: Defining mandatory [=scroll snap areas=] at the initial scroll position and at the [=overscroll area=] ensures that scrolling settles with the [=overscroll area=] either fully open or fully closed, rather than stopping partway in between.

Stacking and Push Translation {#overscroll-stacking-and-push}
-------------------------------------------------------------

When an [=overscroll container=] |container| has [=overscroll areas=] <var>area</var><sub>1</sub>, <var>area</var><sub>2</sub>, &hellip;, <var>area</var><sub><var>n</var></sub> in [=flat tree=] order, with corresponding [=overscroll area parent=] boxes <var>scroller</var><sub>1</sub>, <var>scroller</var><sub>2</sub>, &hellip;, <var>scroller</var><sub><var>n</var></sub>:

1. All [=overscroll area parent=] boxes of |container| are stacked and painted after |container|'s in-flow and positioned descendants, in reverse [=flat tree=] order:
	<var>scroller</var><sub><var>n</var></sub> paints first (immediately above |container|'s regular content), followed by <var>scroller</var><sub><var>n</var>&minus;1</sub>, &hellip;, <var>scroller</var><sub>1</sub>, with <var>scroller</var><sub>1</sub> painting last (on top of all other content in |container|).
2. Transform hierarchy:
	* If |container|'s computed 'overscroll-container-type' is ''overscroll-container-type/overlay'', each <var>scroller</var><sub><var>k</var></sub>'s scroll translation applies only to <var>scroller</var><sub><var>k</var></sub>'s own children (<var>area</var><sub><var>k</var></sub>'s ''::overscroll-backdrop'' and <var>area</var><sub><var>k</var></sub>).
	* If |container|'s computed 'overscroll-container-type' is ''overscroll-container-type/push'' or ''overscroll-container-type/auto'', each <var>scroller</var><sub><var>k</var></sub>'s scroll translation also applies to <var>scroller</var><sub><var>k</var>+1</sub> &hellip; <var>scroller</var><sub><var>n</var></sub> and to |container|'s scrolling content (offset by <var>scroller</var><sub><var>k</var></sub>'s [=scroll origin=]). Consequently, scrolling <var>scroller</var><sub><var>k</var></sub> translates |container|'s regular content and any lower [=overscroll areas=] by the same scroll offset.

Default UA Stylesheet {#ua-stylesheet}
--------------------------------------

Each [=overscroll area parent=] is styled as if the following declarations were applied:

<pre class="lang-css">
display: block;
position: absolute;
inset: 0;
overflow: auto;
overscroll-behavior: inherit;
pointer-events: none;
scroll-behavior: inherit;
scroll-snap-type: pair mandatory;
scrollbar-width: none;
</pre>

User agents must apply the equivalent of the following default stylesheet rules for [=overscroll areas=] and ''::overscroll-backdrop'':

<pre class="lang-css">
[overscrollcontainer] > [overscrollarea]:overscroll-closed {
	interactivity: inert;
}

[overscrollcontainer] > [overscrollarea]::overscroll-backdrop {
	display: none;
	position: absolute;
	inset: 0;
	background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
}

[overscrollcontainer] > [overscrollarea]:overscroll-open::overscroll-backdrop {
	display: block;
}
</pre>


Scroll Chaining and Boundary Default Actions {#scroll-chaining-and-boundary-default-actions}
============================================================================================

Scroll Chaining Order {#scroll-chaining-order}
----------------------------------------------

<dfn>Scroll chaining</dfn> is when scrolling is propagated from one [=scroll container=]
to an ancestor [=scroll container=] following the [=scroll chain=].
Chaining occurs when a [=scroll container=]'s [=scrollport=] has reached its [=scroll boundary=].

<dfn>Scroll boundary</dfn> refers to when the scroll position of a [=scroll container=]
reaches the edge of the [=scrollport=].
If a [=scroll container=] (or [=overscroll container=]) has no potential to scroll
because it does not [=scrollable overflow|overflow=] in the direction of the scroll,
it is always considered to be at the [=scroll boundary=].
A [=single-axis scroll container=] only has a [=scroll boundary=] in its [=scrollable axis=];
if the scroll is in its other axis,
this element is not part of the [=scroll chain=] unless it is an [=overscroll container=] with an [=overscroll area parent=] scrollable in that axis.

A <dfn>scroll chain</dfn> is the order in which scrolling is propagated from one [=scroll container=] to another:

1. For an [=overscroll container=] |container| with [=overscroll areas=] <var>area</var><sub>1</sub>, <var>area</var><sub>2</sub>, &hellip;, <var>area</var><sub><var>n</var></sub> in [=flat tree=] order and corresponding [=overscroll area parent=] boxes <var>scroller</var><sub>1</sub>, <var>scroller</var><sub>2</sub>, &hellip;, <var>scroller</var><sub><var>n</var></sub>, the scroll parent links between |container| and |container|'s nearest ancestor [=scroll container=] |ancestor| are ordered as:
	<p style="text-align: center">|container| &rarr; <var>scroller</var><sub><var>n</var></sub> &rarr; <var>scroller</var><sub><var>n</var>&minus;1</sub> &rarr; &hellip; &rarr; <var>scroller</var><sub>1</sub> &rarr; |ancestor|</p>
	Consequently:
	* A scroll reaching the [=scroll boundary=] of |container| chains in reverse [=flat tree=] (Last-In, First-Out) order: first to <var>scroller</var><sub><var>n</var></sub> (the last [=overscroll area=] in [=flat tree=] order), then to <var>scroller</var><sub><var>n</var>&minus;1</sub>, and so on up to <var>scroller</var><sub>1</sub>, before chaining to |ancestor|.
	* A scroll originating within <var>area</var><sub><var>k</var></sub> (or on <var>area</var><sub><var>k</var></sub>'s ''::overscroll-backdrop'') chains to <var>scroller</var><sub><var>k</var></sub>, then to <var>scroller</var><sub><var>k</var>&minus;1</sub>, &hellip;, <var>scroller</var><sub>1</sub>, and then to |ancestor|.
2. For any other [=scroll container=], the next link in its [=scroll chain=] is the nearest ancestor [=scroll container=] (or [=overscroll container=]) in the [=containing block chain=] that is scrollable in the scroll's axis.
3. The [=viewport=] participates in [=scroll chaining=] as the document's {{Document/scrollingElement}}.

<dfn>Boundary default action</dfn> refers to the user-agent-defined <a>default action</a>
performed when scrolling against the edge of the <a>scrollport</a>.
A <dfn>local boundary default action</dfn> is a <a>boundary default action</a>
which is performed on the <a>scroll container</a> without interacting with the page,
for example displaying a overscroll UI affordance or [=overscroll area=].
Conversely, a <dfn>non-local boundary default action</dfn> interacts with the page,
for example scroll chaining to an ancestor [=scroll container=] or a navigation action.


Overscroll Behavior Properties {#overscroll-behavior-properties}
================================================================

The <dfn>overscroll behavior</dfn> controls the permitted [=boundary default action=] for a
[=scroll container=] or [=overscroll container=] when its [=scrollport=] reaches its [=scroll boundary=].

The 'overscroll-behavior' property specifies the <a>overscroll behavior</a> for a <a>scroll
container</a> element. It allows the content author to specify that a <a>scroll container</a>
element must prevent scroll chaining and/or overscroll affordances.

An element that is neither a [=scroll container=] nor an [=overscroll container=] must accept but ignore 'overscroll-behavior'.
This property must be applied to all scrolling methods supported by the user agent.

Note: This property should provide guarantees that are, at least, as strong as <a>preventDefault</a>
for preventing both scroll chaining and overscroll. Doing otherwise would cause content authors to
use <a>preventDefault</a> instead.


The 'overscroll-behavior' Shorthand {#overscroll-shorthand}
-----------------------------------

<pre class=propdef>
Name: overscroll-behavior
Value: [ contain | none | auto | chain ]{1,2}
Initial: auto auto
Applies to: [=scroll container=] and [=overscroll container=] elements
Inherited: no
Media: visual
Computed value: see individual properties
Animation type: discrete
Canonical order: <abbr title="follows order of property value definition">per grammar</abbr>
</pre>


The 'overscroll-behavior' property is a shorthand property that sets the specified values of
'overscroll-behavior-x' and 'overscroll-behavior-y' in that order. If only one value is specified,
the second value defaults to the same value.

Values have the following meanings:

<dl dfn-for="overscroll-behavior, overscroll-behavior-x, overscroll-behavior-y, overscroll-behavior-inline, overscroll-behavior-block" dfn-type="value">
  <dt><dfn>contain</dfn>
  <dd>
    This value indicates that the element must not perform <a>non-local boundary default actions</a>
    such as scroll chaining or navigation. The user agent must not perform scroll chaining to any
    ancestors along the <a>scroll chain</a> regardless of whether the scroll originated at this
    element or one of its descendants. This value must not modify the behavior of how <a>local
    boundary default actions</a> should behave, such as showing any overscroll affordances.
  <dt><dfn>none</dfn>
  <dd>
    This value implies the same behavior as <a value for=overscroll-behavior>contain</a> and in
    addition this element must also not perform <a>local boundary default actions</a> such as
    showing any overscroll affordances.
  <dt><dfn>chain</dfn>
  <dd>
    This value indicates that the element may perform <a>non-local boundary default actions</a>
    such as scroll chaining or navigation. However, the element must not perform <a>local
    boundary default actions</a>, such as showing any overscroll affordances.
  <dt><dfn>auto</dfn>
  <dd>
    This value indicates that the user agent should perform the usual <a>boundary default action</a>
    with respect to <a>scroll chaining</a>, overscroll and navigation gestures.
</dl>

Note: In the case where a user agent does not implement scroll chaining and overscroll affordances,
these values will have no side effects for a compliant implementation.

Note: Programmatic scrolling is clamped and can not trigger any <a>boundary default actions</a>.

Overscroll and Positioned Elements {#overscroll-positioned}
----------------------------------

This specification does not generally dictate what, if any,
"overscroll" or similar actions
might occur as a [=local boundary default action=].

However, if a user agent <em>does</em> use "overscroll" behavior
(that is, allowing a scrollable element
to be scrolled slightly "past the end" of its scrollable area,
usually with a "rubber-banding" effect after the scroll or drag is completed),
then the following applies:

If an element uses [=fixed positioning=]
and is positioned relative to the [=initial containing block=],
or is a [=sticky positioned=] element
which is currently stuck to the [=viewport=],
then when the [=root scroller=] experiences "overscroll",
that element <em>must not</em> overscroll with the rest of the document's content;
it must instead remain positioned as if the scroller was at its minimum/maximum scroll position,
whichever it will return to when the overscroll is finished.

Even tho this can visually shift the fixed/sticky element
relative to other elements on the page,
it must be treated purely as a visual effect,
and not reported as an actual layout/position change
to APIs such as {{Element/getBoundingClientRect()}}.

Note: This behavior is because fixpos and viewport-stuck stickypos elements
are positioned relative to "the viewport",
which is conceptually above the root scroller
in the page hierarchy
(effectively, it's the [=scroll container=]
holding the root scroller).
Thus, overscrolling the root scroller shouldn't have any effect on them,
just like how an abspos
that is a child of a scroller
but whose abspos containing block is <em>above</em> the scroller
isn't affected by the scroller doing anything at all,
including overscroll.


Physical Longhands for 'overscroll-behavior'  {#overscroll-behavior-longhands-physical}
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

<pre class=propdef>
Name: overscroll-behavior-x, overscroll-behavior-y
Value: contain | none | auto | chain
Initial: auto
Applies to: [=scroll container=] and [=overscroll container=] elements
Inherited: no
Logical property group: overscroll-behavior
Percentages: N/A
Media: visual
Computed value: as specified
Animation type: discrete
Canonical order: <abbr title="follows order of property value definition">per grammar</abbr>
</pre>

The 'overscroll-behavior-x' property specifies the <a>overscroll behavior</a> in the horizontal
axis and the 'overscroll-behavior-y' property specifies the <a>overscroll behavior</a> in the
vertical axis. When scrolling is performed along both the horizontal and vertical axes at the
same time, the <a>overscroll behavior</a> of each respective axis should be considered
independently.


Flow-relative Longhands for 'overscroll-behavior'  {#overscroll-behavior-longhands-logical}
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

<pre class=propdef>
Name: overscroll-behavior-inline, overscroll-behavior-block
Value: contain | none | auto | chain
Initial: auto
Applies to: [=scroll container=] and [=overscroll container=] elements
Inherited: no
Logical property group: overscroll-behavior
Percentages: N/A
Media: visual
Computed value: as specified
Animation type: discrete
Canonical order: <abbr title="follows order of property value definition">per grammar</abbr>
</pre>

These properties correspond to the 'overscroll-behavior-x' and 'overscroll-behavior-y' properties.
The mapping depends on the element's 'writing-mode'.


Overscroll Events {#overscroll-events}
======================================

Event Dispatch {#overscroll-event-dispatch}
-------------------------------------------

Each [=overscroll area=] |area| has a boolean <dfn>overscrolling state</dfn> (initially <code>false</code>) and a boolean <dfn>active overscroll flag</dfn> (initially <code>false</code>).

<table class="data" dfn-type="event" dfn-for="OverscrollEvent">
	<thead>
		<tr>
			<th>Event
			<th>Interface
			<th>Targets
			<th>Description
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<th><dfn>overscrollstart</dfn>
			<td>{{OverscrollEvent}}
			<td>[=overscroll containers=]
			<td>Fired at the [=overscroll container=] when |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=] begins scrolling or changing snap targets while its [=active overscroll flag=] is <code>false</code>.
		<tr>
			<th><dfn>overscrollchanging</dfn>
			<td>{{OverscrollEvent}}
			<td>[=overscroll containers=]
			<td>Fired at the [=overscroll container=] when |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=]'s [=eventual snap target=] changes during an active overscroll.
		<tr>
			<th><dfn>overscrollend</dfn>
			<td>{{OverscrollEvent}}
			<td>[=overscroll containers=]
			<td>Fired at the [=overscroll container=] when the scroll of |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=] completes and its snapped target differs from the target before the scroll started.
		<tr>
			<th><dfn>overscrollcancel</dfn>
			<td>{{OverscrollEvent}}
			<td>[=overscroll containers=]
			<td>Fired at the [=overscroll container=] when the scroll of |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=] completes and its snapped target is unchanged from before the scroll started.
</table>

<div algorithm="enqueue overscrollstart">
	When an [=overscroll area=] |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=] begins a scroll or snap change while |area|'s [=active overscroll flag=] is <code>false</code>:

	1. Set |area|'s [=active overscroll flag=] to <code>true</code>.
	2. Append an {{OverscrollEvent}} named {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollstart}} with target set to |area|'s [=overscroll container=], {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollTarget}} set to |area|, and {{OverscrollEvent/overscrolling}} set to |area|'s [=overscrolling state=] to the document's [=pending scroll events=].
</div>

<div algorithm="enqueue overscrollchanging">
	When an [=overscroll area=] |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=] |scroller| updates its [=eventual snap target=] while |area|'s [=active overscroll flag=] is <code>true</code>:

	1. Let |isOverscrolling| be <code>true</code> if |scroller|'s [=eventual snap target=] is |area|'s [=scroll snap area=], and <code>false</code> otherwise.
	2. Set |area|'s [=overscrolling state=] to |isOverscrolling|.
	3. Append an {{OverscrollEvent}} named {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollchanging}} with target set to |area|'s [=overscroll container=], {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollTarget}} set to |area|, and {{OverscrollEvent/overscrolling}} set to |area|'s [=overscrolling state=] to the document's [=pending scroll events=].
</div>

<div algorithm="enqueue overscroll finished">
	When a scrolling operation on an [=overscroll area=] |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=] |scroller| is <a spec="cssom-view-1" lt="scroll completed">completed</a> while |area|'s [=active overscroll flag=] is <code>true</code>:

	1. Set |area|'s [=active overscroll flag=] to <code>false</code>.
	2. Let |type| be {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollend}} if |scroller|'s realized [=snap target=] changed from the [=snap target=] prior to {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollstart}}, and {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollcancel}} otherwise.
	3. Append an {{OverscrollEvent}} named |type| with target set to |area|'s [=overscroll container=], {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollTarget}} set to |area|, and {{OverscrollEvent/overscrolling}} set to |area|'s [=overscrolling state=] to the document's [=pending scroll events=].
</div>

{{OverscrollEvent}}s do not bubble and are not cancelable.

The {{OverscrollEvent}} Interface {#overscroll-event-interface}
---------------------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary OverscrollEventInit : EventInit {
	Element? overscrollTarget = null;
	boolean overscrolling = false;
};

[Exposed=Window]
interface OverscrollEvent : Event {
	constructor(DOMString type, optional OverscrollEventInit eventInitDict = {});
	readonly attribute Element? overscrollTarget;
	readonly attribute boolean overscrolling;
};
</pre>

<dl dfn-type="attribute" dfn-for="OverscrollEvent">
	: <dfn>overscrollTarget</dfn>
	:: The [=overscroll area=] {{Element}} associated with the event.

	: <dfn>overscrolling</dfn>
	:: The [=overscrolling state=] of {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollTarget}} when the event was queued (<code>false</code> when snapped/snapping to the initial scroll position, <code>true</code> when snapped/snapping to {{OverscrollEvent/overscrollTarget}}).
</dl>


Accessibility Considerations {#accessibility}
=============================================

Gesture-based overscroll interactions are inaccessible when they lack an equivalent non-gesture control or when offscreen content remains in the sequential focus order. This specification addresses both requirements through the following mechanisms:

* Requiring a [=show invoker=] or [=toggle invoker=] in the same [=tree scope=] as a prerequisite for an element to be a valid [=overscroll area=] ensures that every gesture-driven [=overscroll area=] has an associated semantic [=command invoker=] in the DOM:
	* Keyboard users can navigate to the invoker and activate it to [=open=] or [=close the overscroll area=].
	* Screen readers and other assistive technologies perceive a standard activatable button relationship rather than an invisible gesture zone, and authors can apply standard ARIA attributes to the invoker and [=overscroll area=] as needed.
	* The invoker provides a discoverable visual affordance for the overscroll action.
* Applying ''interactivity: inert'' to closed [=overscroll areas=] ('':overscroll-closed'') removes their interactive descendants from sequential focus navigation while closed:
	* <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG20/Understanding/on-focus">WCAG 3.2.1 On Focus</a> requires that receiving focus must not initiate a change of context. If interactive elements inside a closed [=overscroll area=] remained focusable, focusing them would scroll them into view (to satisfy <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-not-obscured-minimum">WCAG 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured</a>) and inadvertently [=open=] the [=overscroll area=]. Making closed [=overscroll areas=] [=inert=] prevents focus traversal from triggering this unexpected context change.
	* Allowing a [=toggle invoker=] inside its closed [=overscroll area=] to escape '':overscroll-closed'' inertness allows a peeking toggle control to remain focusable and operable in both the open and closed states.


Security Considerations {#security}
===================================

There are no known security impacts of this feature. The feature may be used to prevent
certain native UI features such as overscroll affordances and overscroll navigations (e.g., pull-to-refresh,
swipe navigations). However, this does not expose any additional abilities beyond what
is already possible in the platform e.g., by preventing the default action of the event that would
cause a scroll.


Privacy Considerations {#privacy}
=================================

No new privacy considerations have been reported on this specification.


<h2 class="no-num" id="html-spec-changes">Appendix A: HTML Specification Changes</h2>

Content and IDL Attributes {#html-attributes}
---------------------------------------------

The following boolean content attributes are added to HTML elements:

: <dfn export attr-for="html-global">overscrollcontainer</dfn>
:: Designates an element as a potential [=overscroll container=].

: <dfn export attr-for="html-global">overscrollarea</dfn>
:: Designates an element as a potential [=overscroll area=].

<pre class="idl">
partial interface HTMLElement {
	[CEReactions, Reflect] attribute boolean overscrollContainer;
	[CEReactions, Reflect] attribute boolean overscrollArea;
};
</pre>

Command Invokers {#html-invoker-commands}
-----------------------------------------

An <dfn export>overscroll invoker</dfn> is a [=command invoker=] element whose <{button/commandfor}> target is an [=overscroll area=] in the same [=tree scope=] and whose <{button/command}> attribute is one of the following keywords:

<dl dfn-type="attr-value" dfn-for="button/command">
	: <dfn>toggle-overscroll</dfn> (a <dfn dfn-type="dfn" export>toggle invoker</dfn>)
	:: If the target [=overscroll area=] matches '':overscroll-open'', [=close the overscroll area=]; otherwise [=open the overscroll area=].

	: <dfn>show-overscroll</dfn> (a <dfn dfn-type="dfn" export>show invoker</dfn>)
	:: [=Open the overscroll area=].

	: <dfn>hide-overscroll</dfn> (a <dfn dfn-type="dfn" export>hide invoker</dfn>)
	:: [=Close the overscroll area=].
</dl>

<div algorithm="open the overscroll area">
	To <dfn>open the overscroll area</dfn> |area|:

	1. Let |scroller| be |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=].
	2. Let |offset| be the [=scroll snap position=] of |area|'s [=scroll snap area=] in |scroller| along the axis with the larger scroll distance from <code>(0, 0)</code>.
	3. [=Perform a scroll=] of |scroller| to |offset| with behavior set to ''scroll-behavior/auto'', updating |scroller|'s [=snap target=] to |area|'s [=scroll snap area=].
</div>

<div algorithm="close the overscroll area">
	To <dfn>close the overscroll area</dfn> |area|:

	1. Let |scroller| be |area|'s [=overscroll area parent=].
	2. [=Perform a scroll=] of |scroller| to <code>(0, 0)</code> with behavior set to ''scroll-behavior/auto'', updating |scroller|'s [=snap target=] to the initial [=scroll snap area=].
</div>

Inertness {#html-inertness}
---------------------------

A closed [=overscroll area=] (matching '':overscroll-closed'') is made [=inert=] by the user agent default stylesheet via ''interactivity: inert'' (see [[#ua-stylesheet]]), allowing authors to override the closed interactivity using the 'interactivity' property.

In addition, an element |node| inside an [=overscroll container=] |container| is subject to the following [=inert=] rules:

1. If |node| is an [=overscroll area=] of |container| that matches '':overscroll-open'', and another [=overscroll area=] of |container| preceding |node| in [=flat tree=] order also matches '':overscroll-open'', |node| and its [=flat tree=] subtree are [=inert=].
2. If any [=overscroll area=] of |container| matches '':overscroll-open'', every [=flat tree=] child of |container| that is not an [=overscroll area=] (and its [=flat tree=] subtree) is [=inert=].
3. If |node| is a [=toggle invoker=] whose target is an [=overscroll area=] |area| of |container| in the same [=tree scope=], and |node| is a [=flat tree=] descendant of |area|, then while |area| matches '':overscroll-closed'', |node| (and its [=flat tree=] subtree) escapes the ''interactivity: inert'' of |area|, provided |container| itself is not [=inert=] and no [=overscroll area=] of |container| preceding |area| in [=flat tree=] order matches '':overscroll-open''.

Note: Allowing a [=toggle invoker=] to escape the inertness of its closed [=overscroll area=] enables patterns where the toggle button is a child of the [=overscroll area=] that visually peeks into the [=overscroll container=]'s [=scrollport=] while the area is closed, remaining interactive so the user can activate it to [=open=] the area.

Focus and Light Dismiss {#html-focus-and-dismiss}
-------------------------------------------------

* Activating an [=overscroll invoker=] does not change the focused element. Sequential focus navigation (<kbd>Tab</kbd> and <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd>) follows from the [=inert=] state defined in [[#html-inertness]] and HTML's [=sequential focus navigation starting point=] when a focused element becomes [=inert=].
* The default action of a <code>click</code> event on an ''::overscroll-backdrop'' pseudo-element is to [=close the overscroll area=] for every [=overscroll area=] of its [=overscroll container=].

Event Handlers {#html-event-handlers}
-------------------------------------

The following event handlers (and their corresponding event handler event types) must be supported by all HTML elements as both event handler content attributes and event handler IDL attributes on {{GlobalEventHandlers}}, and by all {{Document}} and {{Window}} objects as event handler IDL attributes:

<table class="data" dfn-type=attribute dfn-for="GlobalEventHandlers">
	<thead>
		<tr>
			<th>Event handler
			<th>Event handler event type
	<tbody>
		<tr>
			<td><dfn>onoverscrollstart</dfn>
			<td>{{OverscrollEvent/overscrollstart}}
		<tr>
			<td><dfn>onoverscrollchanging</dfn>
			<td>{{OverscrollEvent/overscrollchanging}}
		<tr>
			<td><dfn>onoverscrollend</dfn>
			<td>{{OverscrollEvent/overscrollend}}
		<tr>
			<td><dfn>onoverscrollcancel</dfn>
			<td>{{OverscrollEvent/overscrollcancel}}
</table>

<pre class="idl">
partial interface mixin GlobalEventHandlers {
	attribute EventHandler onoverscrollstart;
	attribute EventHandler onoverscrollchanging;
	attribute EventHandler onoverscrollend;
	attribute EventHandler onoverscrollcancel;
};
</pre>
